'''wonder''' (wun′dėr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''wonder'', ''wondir'', ''wounder'', ''wunder'', ''wundur'', < AS. ''wundor'' = OS. ''wundar'' = D. ''wonder'' = MLG. ''wunder'' = OHG. ''wuntar'', MHG. G. ''wunder'' = Icel. ''undr'' (for *''vundr'') = Sw. Dan. ''under'', wonder; perhaps akin to Gr. ἀθρεῖν (*Ϝαθρεῖν?), gaze at.] '''1.''' A strange thing; a cause of surprise, astonishment, or admiration; in a restricted sense, a miracle; a marvel, prodigy, or portent.
{{smaller block|Whi thow wratthest the now ''wonder'' me thynketh.
{{right|''Piers Plowman'' (B), iii. 182.}}
<poem>The prophetis seiden with mylde steuene
“A song of ''wondris'' now synge we.”</poem>
{{right|''Hymns to Virgin'', etc. (E. E. T. S.), p. 52.}}
<poem>The love of boys unto their lords is strange;
I have read ''wonders'' of it.</poem>
{{right|''Beau. and Fl.'', Philaster, ii. 1.}}
It is no ''wonder'' that art gets not the victory over nature.
{{right|''Bacon'', Physical Fables, iv., Expl.}}}}

